  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 419 AM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nLong range models continue to adjust their solutions as we approach \nthe weekend period. And while some additional adjustments are likely, \nit seems the consensus is trending towards a surge of moisture and a \nreturn to a more active weather pattern. \n\nAn upper-lvl trough will deepen over the E/SE CONUS, with a series \nof short-wave impulses traveling along its base. These features will \npush some of the deeper moisture southward and into the area, mainly \nbetween Saturday and Monday, and promote increasing instability and \nshower/thunderstorm activity. This will also limit the SAL capping, \nwhich will remain mainly confined to the southern-most areas of \nSoFlo starting on Saturday. Latest NBM follows the rest of guidance \nwith a surge in POPs into the 60-70% range north of I-75, and 30-50% \nelsewhere on Saturday, and with increasing support for strong \nafternoon storms. A similar weather pattern should continue through \nearly next week, although the location of highest POPs may change \ndepending on how the low-lvl flow adjusts.  \n\nTemperatures will remain above normals, with highs generally in the \nlow to mid 90s, and morning lows in the mid 70s over inland South FL \nto around 80 closer to the coasts.",
